  "Question": "\\(y=(3x-1)^2\\). What is the derivative at \\(x=1\\)?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "12",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Method 1: Chain rule\n\nThe chain rule requires multiplying the derivative of the square by the derivative \\(3\\) of the inner function \\(3x-1\\). Thus \\(y'=2(3x-1)\\times3\\). At \\(x=1\\), this gives \\(y'(1)=2\\times2\\times3=12\\).\n\nMethod 2: Expand before differentiating\n\nExpanding the square turns the expression into a polynomial: \\(y=9x^2-6x+1\\). Differentiating term by term gives \\(y'=18x-6\\). Therefore \\(y'(1)=18-6=12\\).",
